(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["product",{"c:choosing-use-cases-with-clear-value":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Good AI use cases sit where a frequent, costly task meets something models do well, and where mistakes are cheap or easy to catch. Score ideas on value, error cost, error detectability, data availability and feasibility before building anything. The best first project is usually \u003cb>assistive\u003c/b>, with a person checking the output, rather than full automation.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Start from the task, not the model\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Map the real workflow first: who does the task, how often, how long it takes, what it costs and where people get stuck. Value is roughly \u003cb>frequency × time per task × number of people × cost of their time\u003c/b>. A feature that saves ten minutes on a task done once a quarter is a demo; one that saves two minutes on a task done 5,000 times a day is a business.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Then ask the question Google's People + AI Guidebook puts first: does AI add unique value here? If clear rules can do the job (routing by a form field, validating a postcode), write code. AI earns its place where inputs are messy language, documents or images, where judgement is needed, or where the space of cases is too large for rules.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Automate or augment?\u003c/b> Automate when the task is tedious, high-volume and low-stakes, and errors are cheap to reverse (tagging, spam, routing). Augment, meaning the AI drafts and a person decides, when stakes are high, when people enjoy or are accountable for the task, or when trust has not been earned yet.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>A scoring rubric\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Criterion\u003c/th>\u003cth>Score 1\u003c/th>\u003cth>Score 5\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Value\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Rare task, minutes saved, few users\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Daily task, hours saved, many users\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Error cost\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A mistake causes harm, lost money or legal exposure\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A mistake is a minor annoyance\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Error detectability\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Wrong answers look right and nobody checks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A person or code can verify quickly\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Data availability\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No examples, no access\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Rich history, documents and labelled outcomes\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Feasibility today\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Needs abilities current models lack\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A quick test with a frontier model already works\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Add the scores with value counted twice (maximum 30). Add one hard rule: if error cost \u003cb>and\u003c/b> detectability are both low, do not automate. Redesign the idea as assistive, or drop it.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Worked example: five ideas for a support organisation\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Idea\u003c/th>\u003cth>Value\u003c/th>\u003cth>Error cost\u003c/th>\u003cth>Detect\u003c/th>\u003cth>Data\u003c/th>\u003cth>Feasible\u003c/th>\u003cth>Total\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Draft replies for agents to edit and send\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4\u003c/td>\u003ctd>5\u003c/td>\u003ctd>5\u003c/td>\u003ctd>5\u003c/td>\u003ctd>27\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Auto-tag and route incoming tickets\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4\u003c/td>\u003ctd>5\u003c/td>\u003ctd>5\u003c/td>\u003ctd>26\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Summarise long threads when a ticket changes hands\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3\u003c/td>\u003ctd>5\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4\u003c/td>\u003ctd>5\u003c/td>\u003ctd>24\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Auto-approve refunds up to $500\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3\u003c/td>\u003ctd>17\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Public chatbot that answers anything about the company\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3\u003c/td>\u003ctd>14\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Draft replies win: a person reviews every output, years of tickets and replies exist, and a test takes a day. Refund automation has value, but errors cost money and are hard to spot, so it waits for rules plus human approval. The open-ended public chatbot is the classic trap: anything it says looks authoritative, and a tribunal has already held a company to its chatbot's wrong promise (the Trust lesson tells that story).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Signs of a good first project\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A measurable baseline exists today: handle time, backlog, error rate.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A named owner feels the pain and will use the feature weekly.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Someone already looks at the output, so review costs little.\u003c/li>\u003cli>You can test feasibility on 30 to 50 real examples within two days.\u003c/li>\u003cli>It shows value without a six-month integration.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Anti-patterns\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>\"Add a chatbot\"\u003c/b> with no specific task behind it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>A solution looking for a problem:\u003c/b> \"we have embeddings, what can we do with them?\"\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Tasks where 95% accuracy is worthless,\u003c/b> such as filing taxes with no review.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>No way to measure success.\u003c/b>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Building what you can buy\u003c/b> (the Build versus buy lesson).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>In practice: the feasibility spike\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Before committing a team, spend one or two days on a spike: collect 30 to 50 real inputs, run the best frontier model with a reasonable prompt, and grade the outputs by hand against what a good answer looks like. If fewer than about half are usable, narrow the scope or rethink. If most are good, you have a candidate, a first eval set, and a list of failure modes to design around. The O'Reilly authors' advice applies: start small, build trust with a narrow, well-defined feature, then widen.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Score these five ideas from 1 to 5 on value, error cost and data availability (higher is better in each), and pick which to build first: (a) drafting replies for support agents; (b) auto-approving refunds; (c) summarising sales calls for reps; (d) extracting clauses from contracts for the legal team; (e) a public chatbot that answers anything about the company.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>(a) Value 4 (thousands of tickets a week), error cost 4 (an agent edits before sending), data 5 (years of tickets and replies): 13 of 15. (b) Value 4, error cost 1 (money leaves the company and fraud is possible), data 3 (past decisions exist but the rules are messy): 8. (c) Value 3 (saves each rep minutes per call), error cost 4 (the rep reviews, harm is low), data 3 (transcripts exist but need consent): 10. (d) Value 4 (lawyer time is expensive), error cost 2 (a missed clause is costly even with review), data 3 (contracts exist, few are labelled): 9. (e) Value 2 (no specific task), error cost 2 (wrong promises can bind the company), data 2: 6. Build (a) first, then (c); run (d) as an assistive tool with lawyer review; automate (b) only behind rules and human approval; replace (e) with a scoped help-centre assistant that cites sources and escalates.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why score error detectability separately from error cost?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A costly error that people reliably catch, such as a draft a lawyer reviews, is manageable. The dangerous combination is high cost with low detectability: plausible but wrong output that nobody checks before it causes harm.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">When should a feature automate rather than augment?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When the task is high-volume, tedious and low-stakes, errors are cheap to detect and reverse, and the system has earned trust through evals and a period of assisted use.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:ux-patterns-for-ai-features":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Chat is one interaction pattern among many, and often not the best one. Choose the pattern from how much autonomy the task can tolerate: inline suggestions, a draft to edit, a structured result with sources, an assistant panel, or a background agent whose work a person reviews. Then design how the feature shows what it used, how sure it is, and how people correct it.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Patterns, from least to most autonomy\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Pattern\u003c/th>\u003cth>Example\u003c/th>\u003cth>Good when\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Inline suggestion\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Code completion, ghost text in an email\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Very frequent, low stakes, accepted with one key\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Transform a selection\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"Shorter\", \"more formal\", \"translate\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The user owns the content and reviews instantly\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Draft for review\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Support reply, pull-request description\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Medium stakes; a person edits and sends\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Structured result with evidence\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Extracted invoice fields with the source text highlighted\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Factual tasks that need verification\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Assistant panel or chat\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A copilot beside a document or app\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Varied, exploratory questions\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Background agent with review\u003c/td>\u003ctd>An agent opens a pull request, or prepares a refund for approval\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Multi-step work with a checkable result\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Full automation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Spam filtering, ticket tagging\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Low error cost, high volume, easy rollback\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>In his 2025 talk \u003ci>Software is changing (again)\u003c/i>, Andrej Karpathy calls this an \u003cb>autonomy slider\u003c/b>: good AI products let users choose how much to hand over, as code editors do when they span tab completion, editing a selection and a full agent mode. Starting low on the slider and moving up as trust grows is a safe product strategy.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Designing for waiting\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Language models are slow next to ordinary UI. Stream text so the first words appear quickly. For agents, show the steps as they happen (\"Searching 3 sources\", \"Reading the refund policy\") or an action plan before execution. Let long jobs run in the background with a notification rather than a spinner. The Cost, latency and reliability module covers the engineering.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Showing sources\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Cite \u003cb>specific passages\u003c/b>, not just document titles: a numbered citation that opens the source scrolled to the highlighted sentence.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For extraction, highlight the span in the original document next to each field, so checking takes seconds.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Show freshness (\"policy updated 3 October\") and scope (\"searched the help centre, not your contracts\").\u003c/li>\u003cli>When nothing relevant was found, say so plainly instead of answering anyway.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Showing confidence\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Do not show the model's own percentage (\"I'm 87% sure\"). A 2023 study of confidence elicitation found that LLMs asked to state their confidence tend to be \u003cb>overconfident\u003c/b>, while agreement across several sampled answers was a more useful signal. Derive confidence from evidence you control instead:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Signal\u003c/th>\u003cth>Example of what the user sees\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Retrieval found no supporting passage\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"I couldn't find this in the policy. Search instead, or ask the team?\"\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Validation failed (schema, totals do not add up)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The field is left empty and marked \"Needs your input\"\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sampled answers disagree\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Two options shown side by side, or a review badge\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>A calibrated classifier score below a threshold\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The item goes to the review queue, not auto-applied\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Coarse labels (\"Verified from 2 sources\", \"Needs review\") are more honest and more useful than precise-looking numbers. Microsoft's guidelines add two expectations to set early: make clear what the system can do, and how well it does it.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Feedback and control\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Thumbs up and down with a few reason chips (\"wrong\", \"outdated\", \"too long\"), so feedback is actionable.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Treat edits as feedback: how much users change a draft is a quality signal (the Observability module shows how to capture it).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Controls for length, tone or strictness, an undo, and a way to turn the feature off.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>In practice: a design review checklist\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>What does the user see in the first second, and while waiting?\u003c/li>\u003cli>How does the user verify the output, and how long does it take?\u003c/li>\u003cli>What happens when the output is wrong (next lesson)?\u003c/li>\u003cli>Where does feedback go, and who reads it?\u003c/li>\u003cli>Is the AI's role disclosed clearly (the Governance module covers disclosure rules)?\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Design how an internal policy assistant shows confidence and sources.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each claim in the answer carries a numbered citation that opens the policy page scrolled to the highlighted passage, with the page title and last-updated date. Confidence comes from evidence, not the model's self-report: if no passage supports the answer it says it could not find it and offers search or a route to the HR team; if sources conflict it shows both; answers resting on an outdated or single weak source carry a \"Check with HR\" label. No percentages are shown, and users can flag an answer as wrong with a reason that goes to the team's review queue.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Which pattern suits an assistant that writes SQL for business analysts, and why?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Draft for review with evidence: the AI writes the query, shows a plain-language explanation and a preview of the results, and the analyst edits or runs it. Wrong SQL can look plausible, so the analyst must see what it does before relying on the numbers.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why avoid showing the model's self-reported confidence percentage?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Verbalised confidence from LLMs tends to be overconfident and is not calibrated to your task, so a precise number creates false trust. Signals you control, such as retrieval coverage, validation results and agreement between samples, are more reliable.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:designing-for-errors-and-recovery":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>An AI feature will be wrong some of the time, so design the wrong path as carefully as the right one: make mistakes easy to notice, cheap to dismiss or correct, and reversible. Microsoft's \u003cb>18 Guidelines for Human-AI Interaction\u003c/b> are a tested checklist, and five of them are specifically about what to do when the system is wrong.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The 18 guidelines\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Microsoft researchers synthesised more than 20 years of work into 18 guidelines, validated through a four-step process and published at CHI 2019. They are grouped by when they apply:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Phase\u003c/th>\u003cth>Guidelines\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Initially\u003c/td>\u003ctd>G1 Make clear what the system can do. G2 Make clear how well the system can do what it can do.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>During interaction\u003c/td>\u003ctd>G3 Time services based on context. G4 Show contextually relevant information. G5 Match relevant social norms. G6 Mitigate social biases.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>When wrong\u003c/td>\u003ctd>G7 Support efficient invocation. G8 Support efficient dismissal. G9 Support efficient correction. G10 Scope services when in doubt. G11 Make clear why the system did what it did.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Over time\u003c/td>\u003ctd>G12 Remember recent interactions. G13 Learn from user behaviour. G14 Update and adapt cautiously. G15 Encourage granular feedback. G16 Convey the consequences of user actions. G17 Provide global controls. G18 Notify users about changes.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The \"when wrong\" group in plain words: let people summon the feature when they want it (G7), wave it away without friction (G8), fix its output without starting over (G9), do less when unsure, for example by asking or offering options instead of guessing (G10), and show the reason behind an output (G11).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Kinds of error\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Google's PAIR guidebook separates \u003cb>system errors\u003c/b> (a wrong answer, a hallucinated fact, a wrong tool action), \u003cb>input errors\u003c/b> (ambiguous or unexpected requests) and \u003cb>context errors\u003c/b> (an answer that is right in general but wrong for this user or moment). For each, weigh how often it happens against how bad it is. Frequent and mild calls for fast correction; rare and severe calls for prevention, confirmation or a human check.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Recovery patterns\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Pattern\u003c/th>\u003cth>Example\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Preview before applying\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Show the diff of an AI edit to a document before it is applied\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Undo and history\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One-click revert of AI changes; versions kept\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Confirm external or destructive actions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Sending an email, paying, deleting: show the exact recipient, amount or record and require an explicit click\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Edit rather than regenerate\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The user fixes one wrong field instead of re-rolling the whole answer\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Ask when unsure\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A clarifying question, or two options, rather than a confident guess\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Graceful degradation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>If the model or provider is down, fall back to search results or a manual form, not an error page\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Explain why\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"Suggested because the receipt says 'Pret A Manger'\" or a link to the source passage\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example: an AI expense categoriser\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Employees photograph receipts; the AI fills merchant, amount, date and category.\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>G8, efficient dismissal:\u003c/b> suggestions appear pre-filled in the normal form fields, not in a modal. Ignoring them costs nothing.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>G9, efficient correction:\u003c/b> changing a category is one click on a dropdown, and each correction is logged as a labelled example for evals and future training.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>G10, scope when in doubt:\u003c/b> if the receipt is blurry or the top two categories score closely, the category is left empty and marked \"Needs your input\" instead of guessed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>G11, explain why:\u003c/b> a small note shows the merchant text that drove the suggestion.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Prevention for the costly field:\u003c/b> the amount is cross-checked against the card transaction; a mismatch blocks submission rather than relying on the user to notice.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Degraded modes and error budgets\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Decide, per error type, what rate you will tolerate and what happens beyond it. A wrong category at 5% may be fine because correction is cheap; a wrong amount at any rate is not, so it gets a deterministic check. Plan the outage path too: timeouts, a fallback model, and a clear message (\"Suggestions are unavailable right now; you can fill this in manually\") so the product keeps working when the AI does not. Agent loops need budgets on steps, time and spend that stop gracefully with a summary of progress.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Run a \"bad day\" review before launch: list the ten most likely wrong outputs from your eval and error analysis, and walk through what the user sees and does for each. If any path ends in confusion, silent harm or a support ticket, fix the design before the model.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Apply three human-AI interaction guidelines to a feature that extracts action items from meeting transcripts.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>G8, efficient dismissal: action items appear as suggestions that can be removed individually with one click, with no confirmation dialog. G9, efficient correction: owner and due date are editable inline, and edits are saved as feedback. G10, scope services when in doubt: when the transcript does not make the owner clear, the item shows \"Owner?\" instead of guessing a name. G11 is a natural fourth: each item links to the transcript timestamp it came from, so the user can check why it was suggested.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is \"edit\" usually a better recovery path than \"regenerate\"?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Regenerating discards the parts that were right and may introduce new errors, so the user must re-check everything. Editing fixes only the wrong part, is faster, and produces a precise correction you can learn from.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What should an AI feature do when its model provider is down?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Degrade gracefully: time out quickly, try a fallback model if one is configured, and otherwise switch to a non-AI path such as search results or a manual form with a short explanation, so the user can still finish the task.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:human-in-the-loop-and-escalation":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep humans in the loop where errors are costly, irreversible or legally significant, and escalate when the system is unsure, stuck, or asked for a person. A good \u003cb>hand-off\u003c/b> passes the full context so the customer never repeats themselves, and every human decision is stored as data that improves the system.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Where people belong: risk tiers\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Tier\u003c/th>\u003cth>Example actions\u003c/th>\u003cth>Human role\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Read-only, low stakes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Answer a how-to question, summarise a thread\u003c/td>\u003ctd>None per request; review samples\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Reversible internal writes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Tag a ticket, draft a reply, open a draft pull request\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Spot checks and review by exception\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>External or costly\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Email a customer, refund under a limit, change account settings\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Approve before execution\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Irreversible or regulated\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Large payments, deletions, credit, medical or legal decisions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A person decides; the AI prepares the case\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Terms you will hear: \u003cb>human in the loop\u003c/b> (a person approves each action), \u003cb>human on the loop\u003c/b> (a person monitors and can intervene), and \u003cb>human out of the loop\u003c/b> (fully automatic). The tier, not the technology, decides which applies. In graph-based agents the approval is an interrupt that pauses the run until someone responds (the Graphs module shows how).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to escalate\u003c/h4>\u003cp>OpenAI's agent guide names two classic triggers: \u003cb>exceeding failure thresholds\u003c/b> (too many retries or steps) and \u003cb>high-risk actions\u003c/b>. In practice teams use these:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>The user asks for a person.\u003c/b> Always honour it, quickly, without making them argue.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Low confidence:\u003c/b> retrieval found nothing, validation failed, a classifier score is below threshold.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>No progress:\u003c/b> two or three turns without resolution, the same question rephrased, rising frustration.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Sensitive topics:\u003c/b> legal threats, safety concerns, complaints about the AI, vulnerable users, VIP accounts.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Limits:\u003c/b> an action above a value limit, or an agent past its step, time or spend budget.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Designing the hand-off\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Trigger detected\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Build hand-off packet\u003c/b>\u003csmall>issue, steps, records\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Route\u003c/b>\u003csmall>right queue and priority\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Tell the customer\u003c/b>\u003csmall>who and how long\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Human resumes with context\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Outcome logged as data\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The \u003cb>hand-off packet\u003c/b> a human receives:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>Who the customer is, and whether identity is verified.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The issue in two sentences.\u003c/li>\u003cli>What the AI tried: tools called, results, answers given.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Relevant records: order, account, plan, recent tickets.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Why it escalated (the trigger) and a suggested next step.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A link to the full transcript.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>The customer hears something like: \"I'm passing you to a specialist. They can see this conversation, so you won't need to repeat anything. Current wait is about 10 minutes.\" Outside staffed hours, offer a ticket with an expected reply time rather than a dead end. Let the human hand routine follow-ups back to the AI.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Making approvals work\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Show the exact action:\u003c/b> the email text and recipient, the refund amount and order, with the evidence the AI used. Approve, edit or reject in one click, with a reason on rejection.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Beware automation bias.\u003c/b> When the AI is usually right, reviewers start rubber-stamping. Track how often reviewers change or reject proposals; a rate near zero for weeks is a warning. Some teams seed occasional known-answer items to check that reviews are real.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Plan capacity.\u003c/b> 10,000 conversations a day with a 15% escalation rate is 1,500 hand-offs; at 6 minutes each that is 9,000 minutes, or 150 agent-hours a day, about 19 people on 8-hour shifts. Set escalation thresholds with the team's capacity in mind, and watch the rate after every model or prompt change.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Close the loop.\u003c/b> Store each decision (approved, edited, rejected, and why) as labelled data for evals, prompt fixes and later fine-tuning.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Metrics\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Escalation rate, time to a human, resolution rate after escalation, repeat contact within 7 days, and satisfaction for escalated versus AI-resolved conversations. A falling escalation rate is only good news if repeat contacts and complaints are not rising.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Design the hand-off from a support agent to a human.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Define triggers: the customer asks for a person, low confidence or failed validation, no progress after two or three turns, sensitive topics, or actions above a limit. On trigger, the agent builds a packet with the verified customer identity, a two-sentence issue summary, what it tried (tools, results, answers), relevant records, the escalation reason, a suggested next step and the transcript link; routes it to the right queue with a priority; and tells the customer who will help, that they will not need to repeat themselves, and the expected wait, or offers a ticket outside staffed hours. The human resumes with full context, can hand routine follow-up back to the AI, and the outcome and reason are logged as labelled data. Track escalation rate, time to human, resolution after escalation and repeat contacts.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your agent handles 4,000 chats a day and escalates 20% of them. A human needs 8 minutes per escalated chat. How much staffing does that need?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>4,000 × 0.20 = 800 escalations; 800 × 8 = 6,400 minutes, about 107 agent-hours a day, or roughly 13 to 14 people on 8-hour shifts before allowing for breaks and peaks.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Reviewers approve 99.8% of AI-proposed refunds. Why might that be a problem, and what do you do?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It may mean automation bias: reviewers trust the AI and stop checking. Measure review time and edit rates, seed occasional known-bad proposals to test attention, and if reviews are not adding value, either redesign them to focus on risky cases or adjust the tier with evidence from evals.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:product/trust-sources-confidence-and-limits":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>The goal is \u003cb>calibrated trust\u003c/b>: users rely on an AI feature exactly as much as it deserves, neither ignoring good output nor accepting bad output unchecked. You build it by being clear about what the system can and cannot do, showing where answers come from, admitting uncertainty, behaving consistently, and keeping binding commitments out of the model's hands, because you are responsible for what your AI says.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Over-trust and under-trust\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003cb>Over-trust\u003c/b> (also called automation bias) means people accept wrong output because it is fluent and usually right; the harm lands on users and on you. \u003cb>Under-trust\u003c/b> means people ignore or redo good output, so the feature costs money and delivers nothing. Both are design failures. The jagged frontier study in the Landscape module showed the over-trust risk directly: consultants using AI on a task just outside its abilities did worse than those working alone.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>A case to remember: Air Canada\u003c/h4>\u003cp>In 2022, Air Canada's website chatbot told Jake Moffatt he could book a full-fare flight to his grandmother's funeral and claim the bereavement discount afterwards. The actual policy required the request before travel, and the airline refused the refund. In the dispute, Air Canada argued the chatbot was \"a separate legal entity that is responsible for its own actions\". In February 2024 the British Columbia Civil Resolution Tribunal rejected that argument and ordered the airline to pay Moffatt $812.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The lessons are product decisions, not model choices:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>You own what the AI says.\u003c/b> Customers, courts and regulators treat it as your statement.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Ground policy answers in the policy text\u003c/b> and link to it, so the answer and the source can be compared.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Scope the assistant.\u003c/b> Questions about money, eligibility and exceptions route to a lookup tool or a person.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Keep commitments deterministic.\u003c/b> Prices, refunds, eligibility and exceptions come from systems of record, never from free text the model composes.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Six trust builders\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Set expectations early.\u003c/b> Say what the feature does, which data it uses and its known limits; explain the benefit, not the technology. Microsoft's guidelines G1 and G2: make clear what the system can do and how well.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Show sources\u003c/b> at the level of the passage (the UX patterns lesson).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Admit uncertainty.\u003c/b> Have a real \"I don't know\" path that hands over to search or a person, and use evidence-based signals rather than self-reported confidence.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Be consistent.\u003c/b> The same question should get the same answer: pin model versions, keep temperature low for factual tasks, run regression evals, and tell users when behaviour changes (G18).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Disclose AI involvement.\u003c/b> Label AI-generated content and make it clear when a user is talking to an AI; some jurisdictions require it (the Governance module).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Respect data.\u003c/b> Say what is stored and whether it is used for training, and offer opt-outs.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Scoping limits in practice\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A scoped assistant states its job and routes the rest:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>I can help with orders, delivery and returns.\nFor billing disputes I'll connect you with the billing team; they can see this chat.\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Behind that sentence sit an intent classifier or routing step that catches out-of-scope requests before generation, retrieval limited to approved sources, refusal templates with a route onward, and tool calls for anything that commits the company.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Measuring trust\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Trust is observable in behaviour:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Acceptance and edit rates:\u003c/b> near 100% acceptance with zero edits on a task you know has errors suggests over-trust; very low acceptance suggests under-trust.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Verification behaviour:\u003c/b> how often users open citations. If nobody ever does, either they over-trust or the citations are hidden.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Overrides and escalations\u003c/b> after AI answers.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Complaints and short surveys\u003c/b> (\"The answers are reliable\": agree or disagree), tracked over time.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>When trust breaks publicly, acknowledge it, fix the cause, show what changed, and consider narrowing the feature's scope until evals prove the fix.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What went wrong in the Air Canada chatbot case, and which design choices would have prevented it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The chatbot stated a bereavement-fare rule that contradicted the real policy, the customer relied on it, and the tribunal held the airline responsible, rejecting its claim that the bot was a separate entity. Grounding answers in the policy text with a link, scoping the bot so eligibility and refund questions go to a policy lookup or a person, and never letting free text make commitments would have prevented it.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does calibrated trust mean, and how can you tell if users over-trust a feature?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Users rely on the AI to the degree its real accuracy justifies, per task. Signs of over-trust include near-total acceptance without edits on tasks with known error rates, users never opening sources, and errors reaching customers that a quick check would have caught.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why should commitments such as prices and refunds go through deterministic systems?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Free text from a model can be wrong or manipulated by prompt injection, yet the company may be held to it. Taking values from systems of record through tools makes commitments correct, auditable and consistent with policy.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:rollouts-shadow-mode-canaries-and-a-b-tests":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Never switch an AI change on for everyone at once. Run it in \u003cb>shadow mode\u003c/b> first (on real traffic, invisible to users), then expose a small \u003cb>canary\u003c/b> slice with automatic stop criteria, then run an \u003cb>A/B test\u003c/b> that measures the user outcome. Write the success metric, guardrails, stop criteria and sample size before you start.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Why AI changes need staged rollouts\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A new prompt, model, retrieval setting or tool can shift behaviour in ways offline evals miss: real inputs are messier than your test set, costs and latency change with real lengths, and users react in ways no eval predicts. Offline evals (the Evals module) are the gate before rollout. Online stages catch the rest.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Offline evals pass\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Shadow\u003c/b>\u003csmall>real traffic, hidden\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Canary\u003c/b>\u003csmall>1-5% of users\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>A/B test\u003c/b>\u003csmall>randomised split\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Full rollout\u003c/b>\u003csmall>monitor, keep a hold-out\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The stages\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Stage\u003c/th>\u003cth>What happens\u003c/th>\u003cth>What you learn\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Shadow\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The new version runs on copies of real requests; outputs are logged and compared, never shown\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Quality on real inputs (judge or pairwise comparison), latency, cost, error rates, which tools it would have called\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Internal use\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Employees use it for real work\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Rough edges, obvious failures, missing features\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Canary\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1 to 5% of users, or one region or customer tier\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Guardrail metrics and incidents on real users, with a small blast radius\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>A/B test\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Randomised split, often 50/50\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The causal effect on the outcome that matters\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Ramp\u003c/td>\u003ctd>25%, 50%, 100%, optionally keeping a 5% hold-out\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Long-term effects and drift\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Shadow mode has three catches: tool calls must be stubbed or run in dry-run mode so nothing real happens, it roughly doubles inference cost for the traffic you copy, and it cannot measure how users react.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Success and stop criteria\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Write them down and get them agreed before launch, for example for a new support-assistant model:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Primary metric:\u003c/b> verified resolution rate (no reopen within 7 days).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Guardrails, must not worsen beyond a threshold:\u003c/b> satisfaction score, repeat contacts, escalation rate, p95 latency, cost per conversation.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Automatic stop and roll back:\u003c/b> error rate above 2%, p95 latency above 6 seconds, cost per conversation 30% above control, or any severe safety or privacy incident.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Wire stop criteria into alerts and the feature flag so rollback is a click, or automatic, not a deployment.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How big must an A/B test be?\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For a rate metric such as resolution, the sample per variant is approximately:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>n ≈ (z_alpha/2 + z_beta)² × [p1(1 − p1) + p2(1 − p2)] / (p1 − p2)²\n5% significance: z = 1.96      80% power: z = 0.84\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Baseline rate\u003c/th>\u003cth>Rate you want to detect\u003c/th>\u003cth>Users per variant\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>30%\u003c/td>\u003ctd>35%\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1,374\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>30%\u003c/td>\u003ctd>33%\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3,760\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>20%\u003c/td>\u003ctd>22%\u003c/td>\u003ctd>6,507\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>60%\u003c/td>\u003ctd>65%\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1,468\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Halving the effect you want to detect roughly quadruples the sample. Rules that keep tests honest: randomise by user, not by request, so one person sees one variant; run whole weeks to cover weekly cycles; and do not stop the moment the result looks good, because repeated peeking inflates false positives. Experimentation platforms offer sequential tests designed for safe early looks.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Twists specific to AI\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Higher variance:\u003c/b> non-deterministic outputs add noise, so plan for larger samples.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Novelty effects:\u003c/b> usage of a new AI feature often spikes and then settles; judge on later weeks.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Shared queues:\u003c/b> if AI escalations land on the same human team as the control group, the arms interfere.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cost is a metric:\u003c/b> a variant can win on quality and lose on margin.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Pinned versions:\u003c/b> pin the model version in both arms so a provider update does not change the control mid-test.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Online evals:\u003c/b> run an LLM judge on sampled traffic from both arms as an extra quality signal.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Teams use feature-flag and experimentation tools (LaunchDarkly, Statsig, GrowthBook, Unleash, or in-house flags) keyed on prompt version and model id, and log the variant on every trace so dashboards can split by it (the Observability module).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Plan a staged rollout, with success and stop criteria, for switching a support assistant to a new model and prompt.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Gate on offline evals passing. Run shadow mode for about a week on copied traffic with tools in dry-run, comparing answers with a pairwise judge and checking latency and cost. Then a 5% canary for a few days with automatic rollback if errors exceed 2%, p95 latency exceeds 6 seconds, cost per conversation rises more than 30%, or any severe safety or privacy incident occurs. Then a 50/50 A/B test randomised by user, run for whole weeks until the pre-computed sample is reached, with verified resolution rate as the primary metric and satisfaction, repeat contacts, escalations, latency and cost as guardrails. Ramp to 100% if the primary metric improves and no guardrail breaches, keep a small hold-out for a month, and log the variant on every trace.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your resolution rate is 40%. How many users per variant do you need to detect a rise to 44% at 5% significance and 80% power?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>(1.96 + 0.84)² × (0.40 × 0.60 + 0.44 × 0.56) / 0.04² ≈ 7.85 × 0.486 / 0.0016 ≈ 2,390 users per variant, so about 4,800 in total.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why can shadow mode not replace an A/B test?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Users never see shadow outputs, so it cannot measure their behaviour or the business outcome. It shows quality, cost and safety on real inputs, but only a randomised test shows causal impact on users.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:measuring-business-impact":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Measure AI features by what changes for the business and its users, meaning tasks completed, time saved, quality, revenue or cost, not by model scores or usage counts. Build a \u003cb>metric tree\u003c/b> from the business outcome down to signals you can log, compare against a baseline or a control group, and never rely on self-reported time savings alone.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The metric tree\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Business outcome:  support cost per resolved ticket falls while satisfaction holds\n  Task success:    % of conversations resolved by the AI, verified (no reopen within 7 days)\n  Time saved:      agent handle time on AI-assisted tickets versus a control group\n  Quality:         satisfaction score, reopen rate, policy errors found in weekly audits\n  Adoption:        % of agents using drafts; % of drafts sent with light edits\n  Cost:            AI cost per conversation and per verified resolution\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Every line needs a definition, a data source and an owner. Model metrics (judge scores, eval pass rates) are leading indicators that sit under task success, not substitutes for it.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Defining task success\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Success must be \u003cb>verifiable\u003c/b>, not merely asserted:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Resolved:\u003c/b> the customer did not reopen or contact again within 7 days, not \"the chat ended\".\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Accepted:\u003c/b> a draft was sent with less than a set amount of editing, not \"the user clicked Insert\".\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Correct:\u003c/b> a weekly random audit by a domain expert agrees with the output.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Watch \u003cb>deflection\u003c/b> in particular. A chatbot \"deflects\" a ticket when the user leaves without contacting a human, which also happens when the user gives up. Only resolution, checked against later contacts, measures value.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Time saved: measure it, do not ask for it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>People are poor judges of their own speed with AI. In METR's 2025 randomised trial, 16 experienced open-source developers did 246 tasks in their own repositories, each randomly allowed or disallowed AI tools. They forecast a 24% speed-up and afterwards believed they had been 20% faster; measured completion times were 19% \u003ci>slower\u003c/i>. (METR published newer data on later tools in 2026; the point here is the method.)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Other settings show real gains: in a 2023 controlled experiment, developers with GitHub Copilot built an HTTP server 55.8% faster, and a study of 5,172 customer-support agents found AI assistance raised issues resolved per hour by about 15% on average, most for less experienced agents. Effects depend on the task and the people, so measure in your setting:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Randomised control:\u003c/b> feature on or off per user or per task.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Difference in differences:\u003c/b> compare before and after for a team that got the feature against a similar team that did not.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Time on task from logs,\u003c/b> not surveys.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Sampled audits\u003c/b> to check that speed did not cost quality.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Worked example: return on a support assistant\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Volume: 20,000 tickets a month; fully loaded human cost $6 per ticket\nAI resolves 35% end to end (verified, no reopen in 7 days) = 7,000 tickets\nHuman cost avoided:  7,000 × $6                    = $42,000 a month\nAI cost:             20,000 conversations × $0.15  =  $3,000 a month (it sees every ticket)\nReview and escalation overhead (assumed 0.5 FTE)   =  $3,500 a month\nNet benefit: $42,000 − $3,000 − $3,500             = $35,500 a month\nGuardrails: satisfaction on AI-resolved tickets within 2 points of human-resolved; reopen rate no higher\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>If 10% of the \"resolved\" tickets come back, true resolutions fall to 6,300 and the benefit drops by $4,200. That is why the definition of success matters more than the arithmetic.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Reporting\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A useful weekly one-pager shows the outcome metric against control or baseline with a confidence interval, the guardrails, cost per task, adoption, and the top failure modes with the fixes in progress. Hamel Husain's advice for AI roadmaps applies to reporting too: count experiments run and what they taught, since progress in AI products comes from many small measured iterations rather than features shipped.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Define task success, time saved and quality metrics for an AI feature that drafts replies for support agents.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Task success: the share of tickets where the agent sent the AI draft with light edits (edit distance under a set threshold) and the customer did not reopen within 7 days. Time saved: median handle time on tickets with drafts versus a randomised control group without drafts, measured from logs. Quality: satisfaction and reopen rate for drafted versus control tickets, plus a weekly expert audit of 50 random drafted replies for correctness and policy compliance. Add adoption (share of agents using drafts) and cost per drafted ticket alongside.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why not rely on users' own estimates of time saved?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Perception is unreliable: in METR's randomised trial, developers believed AI made them about 20% faster while measured times were 19% slower. Use logged time on task with a control group.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is the difference between deflection and resolution?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Deflection counts conversations that did not reach a human, including users who gave up; resolution counts problems actually solved, verified by the absence of repeat contact. Only resolution measures value.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:build-versus-buy":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Build what differentiates you and buy what does not. For each component weigh differentiation, data sensitivity, time to value, total cost of ownership, skills and lock-in. Most teams buy models and commodity infrastructure, self-host open source where they need control, and build the product layer: their data, context, workflows, evals and UX.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Three options, not two\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Buy\u003c/b> a managed service or a finished product: fastest, least control, per-unit fees.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Adopt open source and run it yourself\u003c/b> (LiteLLM, Langfuse, vLLM, Postgres with pgvector): control and no licence fees, but you operate it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Build\u003c/b> from scratch: maximum fit, maximum maintenance. Justified only where it is your advantage or nothing suitable exists.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>The decision factors\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Factor\u003c/th>\u003cth>Leans build or self-host\u003c/th>\u003cth>Leans buy\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Differentiation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Core to why customers choose you\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Commodity, the same for everyone\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Data sensitivity\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Data cannot leave your network or region\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Vendor meets your compliance and retention terms\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Time to value\u003c/td>\u003ctd>You can wait months\u003c/td>\u003ctd>You need results this quarter\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Total cost of ownership\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Volume is high, so per-unit fees dominate\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Volume is modest, so engineers cost more than fees\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Skills\u003c/td>\u003ctd>You have people to run it on call\u003c/td>\u003ctd>You would need to hire\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Change and lock-in\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fast-moving area where you need control\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Stable standards and easy exit\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Typical choices in 2026\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Component\u003c/th>\u003cth>Usual choice\u003c/th>\u003cth>Reason\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Foundation model\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Buy API access, or use open weights\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Training your own almost never pays for a product team\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Gateway and routing\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Open source (LiteLLM) or a managed gateway\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Commodity, but you want control of keys, budgets and fallbacks\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Vector search\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Managed vector database, or pgvector in Postgres you already run\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Commodity; operations burden decides\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Observability and evals tooling\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Buy (LangSmith, Braintrust) or self-host (Langfuse, Phoenix)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Data residency usually decides\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Document parsing and OCR\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Usually buy\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Hard to do well, not differentiating\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Guardrails\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Mix: provider moderation, open classifiers, your own rules\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Policies are yours; detectors are commodity\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Prompts, context assembly, retrieval logic, eval data, UX\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Build\u003c/td>\u003ctd>This is your product\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>A whole feature outside your core (for example a support agent for a company that does not sell support)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Often buy\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The vendor spreads its R&amp;D across thousands of customers\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The O'Reilly team behind \u003ci>What we learned from a year of building with LLMs\u003c/i> sums it up: don't build LLM features you can buy, and the model isn't the product, the system around it is.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked example: build or buy a support AI agent\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Buy:   a vendor charging $0.99 per resolution (Intercom's published Fin price, per outcome, as of late 2026)\n       7,000 resolutions a month × $0.99                       =  $6,930 a month, plus seats\nBuild: 1.5 engineers to build and run it (assumed)             ≈ $25,000 a month\n       observability, evals and other tooling                  ≈  $1,000 a month\n       inference: $0.15 per conversation, 35% resolve,\n       so about $0.43 per resolution × 7,000                   ≈  $3,000 a month\n       total                                                   ≈ $29,000 a month\nBreak-even: 0.99 × R = 26,000 + 0.43 × R   →   R ≈ 46,000 resolutions a month\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Below about 46,000 resolutions a month, buying is cheaper on these assumptions; above it, building starts to pay, if the team can match the vendor's quality. The numbers are illustrative; the method is to put fixed costs, per-unit costs and volume on one line.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Hidden costs on both sides\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Build:\u003c/b> maintenance, on-call, migrating to new models every few months, eval upkeep, security reviews, and opportunity cost of what the team is not building.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Buy:\u003c/b> per-unit fees that grow with success, limited customisation, the vendor's choice of model, data-processing terms, exit costs (exporting conversations and configuration), and vendor risk. Platforms change: OpenAI's 2026 wind-down of self-serve fine-tuning is a reminder.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Keep the decision reversible\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Whatever you choose, own your traces, eval sets and labelled data; prefer standard interfaces (OpenAI-compatible model APIs, MCP for tools, OpenTelemetry for traces) so components can be swapped; and revisit decisions yearly, because prices and capabilities move fast.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Decide build, buy or open source for three platform components at a company that must keep customer data in the EU: an LLM gateway, tracing and observability, and invoice parsing.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Gateway: self-host an open-source gateway such as LiteLLM in the EU region; it is commodity software, but you need control of keys, budgets, routing and where requests go. Observability: self-host an open-source platform such as Langfuse or Phoenix in the EU, or buy a vendor that offers EU hosting and acceptable retention, because traces contain customer data. Invoice parsing: buy a document-AI service with EU processing, since high-quality parsing is hard and not differentiating, keeping your own eval set so you can compare vendors and switch.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A vendor charges $1.20 per resolution. Building would cost $30,000 a month fixed plus $0.40 per resolution. At what monthly volume does building break even?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>1.20 × R = 30,000 + 0.40 × R, so 0.80 × R = 30,000 and R = 37,500 resolutions a month.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Which parts should a product team almost always build itself?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The parts that encode its own knowledge and customers: prompts and context assembly, retrieval over its data, workflow and tool integrations, eval datasets and the user experience.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:product/pricing-ai-features-cost-to-serve-and-margins":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Every AI request has a marginal cost, so unlike classic software, a heavy user can cost more than they pay. Price from the \u003cb>cost to serve\u003c/b>: model the cost per task, its distribution across users and the margin you need, then pick a pricing model (seat with allowance, usage, credits or outcome-based) that keeps power users profitable.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Cost per task\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Cost per task is the sum over every model call of input and output tokens times their prices, minus caching discounts, plus retrieval, tools and infrastructure (the Billing module covers the details). Agents multiply calls, so they multiply cost.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>One agent task: 15 model calls, each 8,000 input + 400 output tokens, at $3 / $15 per million\n  no caching:  15 × (8,000 × 3 + 400 × 15) / 1,000,000                  = $0.45 per task\n  80% of input read from cache at a tenth of the input price:\n               15 × (1,600 × 3 + 6,400 × 0.30 + 400 × 15) / 1,000,000   ≈ $0.19 per task\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The distribution matters more than the average\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A $40-a-month seat, with 60% light users (10 tasks a month), 30% regular (60) and 10% power users (400):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Segment\u003c/th>\u003cth>Share of users\u003c/th>\u003cth>Tasks a month\u003c/th>\u003cth>Cost, no caching\u003c/th>\u003cth>Cost, with caching\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Light\u003c/td>\u003ctd>60%\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$4.50\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$1.91\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Regular\u003c/td>\u003ctd>30%\u003c/td>\u003ctd>60\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$27.00\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$11.45\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Power\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10%\u003c/td>\u003ctd>400\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$180.00\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$76.32\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Average per seat\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$28.80\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$12.21\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Inference gross margin on the seat is (40 − 28.80) / 40 = 28% without caching and 69% with it. Even with caching, a power user costs $76 against $40 of revenue, and the 10% of power users generate 62% of all inference cost. Averages hide this. In 2020, a16z observed that AI companies often ran gross margins of 50 to 60%, against 60 to 80% or more for comparable SaaS businesses, partly because of compute costs; agentic products make the distribution even more skewed.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pricing models\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Model\u003c/th>\u003cth>How it works\u003c/th>\u003cth>Strength\u003c/th>\u003cth>Weakness\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Flat seat\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Per user per month\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Simple, predictable\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Heavy users erode margin\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Seat with allowance and overage\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Included tasks, then metered\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Protects margin, mostly predictable\u003c/td>\u003ctd>More to explain\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Usage or credits\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Pay per task, token or credit\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Price tracks cost\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Unpredictable bills; users ration use\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Outcome-based\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Pay per resolved outcome\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Price tracks value\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Defining and verifying outcomes; disputes\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Real examples move between these. Intercom prices its Fin agent per outcome ($0.99 as of late 2026) on top of seats. GitHub Copilot began billing metered \"premium requests\" with per-model multipliers in June 2025 and changed its billing again in 2026. Expect to revise pricing as usage patterns and model prices change.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Fixing the example with an allowance\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Include 100 tasks in the $40 seat and charge $0.50 per extra task. Light and regular users pay $40; a power user pays $40 + 300 × $0.50 = $190 against $76 of cost. Average revenue per seat rises to $55 against $12.21 of cost, a 78% inference margin, and no segment loses money.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Other levers\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Reduce cost:\u003c/b> caching, routing easy steps to small models, fine-tuned small models, batch processing for non-urgent work, and step limits on agents (the Reliability and Fine-tuning modules).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Shape usage:\u003c/b> fair-use limits, soft caps with upgrade prompts, cheaper \"fast\" and pricier \"deep\" modes that users choose.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Watch margins per customer:\u003c/b> cost dashboards by account, alerts when a customer's cost exceeds their revenue, and a pricing review whenever model prices change. Prices per token have tended to fall, so decide whether to pass savings on or reinvest them in quality.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A feature costs $0.02 per task and users pay $20 a month. Light users run 100 tasks a month and heavy users 3,000. What is the inference margin for each, and how would you fix the loss?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Light users cost $2, a 90% margin. Heavy users cost $60 against $20, a loss of $40 each (a −200% margin). Include an allowance, for example 1,000 tasks, then charge above cost, say $0.03 per extra task: heavy users then pay $20 + 2,000 × $0.03 = $80 against $60 of cost, a 25% margin. Also cut cost per task with caching and routing.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What are the strengths and risks of outcome-based pricing?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It aligns price with the value customers get and is easy to justify. The risks are defining and verifying the outcome (did the ticket really get resolved?), disputes over what counts, and revenue that depends on your AI's success rate.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why do AI products tend to have lower gross margins than classic SaaS?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each request consumes paid compute, so cost grows with usage, and heavy users or agentic workflows can cost more than they pay; classic SaaS has near-zero marginal cost per action.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","lab:product/a-one-page-ai-feature-spec":"\u003cp>You will write the one-page spec that strong teams agree before building an AI feature. Use a feature from your work, or Atlas.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Pick one feature and one user. Write the problem in two sentences without mentioning AI.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Copy the template and fill every section in a sentence or two. Put a number wherever the template asks for one, even if it is an estimate marked as such.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Feature:            ...\nUser and task:      who, how often, how long today, cost of the task\nWhy AI:             why rules, search or a form would not do the job\nPattern:            inline / draft for review / structured result / assistant / agent with review / automation\nSuccess metric:     one primary metric with its definition and today's baseline\nGuardrails:         2-4 metrics that must not get worse, with thresholds\nTop 3 errors:       for each, how the user notices it and how they recover\nHumans:             what needs approval; escalation triggers; who receives hand-offs\nTrust:              how sources and uncertainty are shown; what is disclosed\nEval plan:          dataset (size, source), metrics, the bar to launch\nRollout:            shadow -&gt; canary % -&gt; A/B; stop criteria; sample size\nCost and price:     cost per task, monthly volume, cost at p90 user, pricing model\nRisks and open questions: ...\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"3\">\u003cli>Run a mini feasibility spike: try 10 real inputs on a frontier model with a quick prompt and note how many outputs are usable. Put the number in the eval plan.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Estimate the cost per task from those 10 runs' token usage and your provider's prices; multiply up to monthly volume and to your heaviest user.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Review the spec against this checklist, or swap with a colleague: is the success metric verifiable? Does every top error have a recovery path? Are stop criteria numeric? Would a power user be profitable?\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>A filled example, abbreviated:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Feature:         reply drafts for support agents\nUser and task:   40 agents, ~120 tickets each per day, median handle time 7 minutes\nWhy AI:          replies need the ticket's specifics and policy text; templates cover only ~30% of cases\nPattern:         draft for review; agent edits and sends; nothing is sent automatically\nSuccess metric:  share of tickets sent with a lightly edited draft and not reopened in 7 days (baseline 0%)\nGuardrails:      satisfaction within 2 points of control; reopen rate no higher; p95 draft time under 4 s\nTop 3 errors:    wrong policy cited (citation links to source; one-click \"wrong policy\" flag);\n                 invented order details (fields filled only from tool lookups, shown highlighted);\n                 wrong tone (tone toggle; edits logged as feedback)\nRollout:         1 week shadow, then 10% of agents, then 50/50 by agent for 3 weeks; stop if reopen rate +2 points\nCost and price:  ~$0.01 per draft; 4,800 drafts a day is ~$48 a day, ~$1,000-1,500 a month; internal tool\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A single page that a product manager, an engineer and a support lead can all read and challenge.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Gaps you had not noticed, typically a success metric that cannot be verified, an error with no recovery path, or no stop criteria.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A cost estimate that makes the build-or-buy and pricing conversation concrete.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>","b:ai-feature-launch-plan":"\u003ch4>The brief\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Turn a feature idea into a launch plan that a real team could execute and a sceptical reviewer would approve. Use a feature from your work, or take the optional Atlas route: starting point Atlas after M14 (assistant, retrieval, actions, evals, tracing, security and gateway in place); this extension adds the launch plan for opening Atlas to its first real users.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Requirements\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A problem statement with the user, the task, today's baseline and the value estimate\u003c/li>\u003cli>A use-case score using the rubric from this module, with the automate-or-augment decision justified\u003c/li>\u003cli>UX for errors: the interaction pattern, the five most likely errors from a real error analysis, and the recovery path for each\u003c/li>\u003cli>A human-in-the-loop design: risk tiers, escalation triggers, the hand-off packet and staffing estimate\u003c/li>\u003cli>An eval baseline: dataset, metrics and launch bar, with current scores\u003c/li>\u003cli>A rollout plan: shadow, canary and A/B stages with success metric, guardrails, numeric stop criteria and a sample-size calculation\u003c/li>\u003cli>A cost-to-serve model with per-segment margins and a pricing or budget recommendation\u003c/li>\u003cli>A post-launch review template for weeks 1, 4 and 12\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Milestones\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Write the problem statement and score the use case (45 min).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Run error analysis on 30 to 50 real or realistic outputs and list the top failure modes (1 h).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Design the UX for errors and the human-in-the-loop flow, including the hand-off packet (1 h).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Record the eval baseline and set the launch bar (45 min).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Write the rollout plan with stop criteria and the sample-size estimate (45 min).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Build the cost model in a spreadsheet or script, by user segment, and recommend pricing or a budget (45 min).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Write the post-launch review template: metrics to read, questions to answer, decisions to take (30 min).\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Done when\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>The plan fits in about five pages and every metric has a definition, a baseline and a threshold\u003c/li>\u003cli>Every top failure mode has a detection method and a recovery path\u003c/li>\u003cli>Stop criteria are numeric and wired, or ready to wire, to a feature flag\u003c/li>\u003cli>The cost model shows the margin for the heaviest 10% of users\u003c/li>\u003cli>A reviewer who has not seen the project could make a go or no-go decision from it\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Stretch goals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Run the shadow stage for real on a day of replayed traffic and add the results\u003c/li>\u003cli>Add a build-versus-buy comparison against one vendor with a break-even volume\u003c/li>\u003cli>Draft the user-facing onboarding copy that sets expectations and discloses AI involvement\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>"}]);
